{"version":1,"ops":[{"type":6,"author":{"id":"a29d5859530e551c9c63051e6b73ecbf9e195803"},"timestamp":1577512723,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDE1OlVzZXJDb250ZW50RWRpdDMzMDQ4ODIzNQ=="},"target":"81f78f3cd6d3746a6f6024a14fddf43e7fd271d88550e7d560b29cde2f0ca0d5","message":"#### What is the problem?\nSuppose there two clones of a datalad superdataset in A and B, both having the subdatasets 1,2,3. If B adds a new subdataset 4, and A adds B's location as a sibling and runs `datalad update --merge --sibling B`, what happens is that A will now have subdataset 4, but cannot get the actual data. To get the data you normally run `datalad get --source B`, but this would fail because the new subdataset 4 does not have B as a remote in A. The workaround is to add manually the remote B on the newly pulled subdataset 4 inside A, then try `get` again. \n\n#### What steps will reproduce the problem?\nSorry, no code for now, above explanation should help create the situation.\n\n#### What version of DataLad are you using (run `datalad --version`)? On what operating system (consider running `datalad wtf`)?\n0.12.0rc6 from Github on Dec 2019\n\n#### Is there anything else that would be useful to know in this context?\nThough this is not a serious bug, it is confusing to the user. Moreover, it is problematic when there hundreds of subdatasets. Running `sibling add` one by one is not reasonable, so I usually go for `sibling add --recursive` from the parent dataset. This in turn throws lots of errors for existing remotes in old subdatasets, but fixes the new ones too. The point is that the process is not smooth and shows errors. Maybe datalad can handle this situation automatically.\n\n#### Have you had any success using DataLad before? (to assess your expertise/prior luck.  We would welcome your testimonial additions to https://github.com/datalad/datalad/wiki/Testimonials as well)\nNA","files":null},{"type":6,"author":{"id":"a29d5859530e551c9c63051e6b73ecbf9e195803"},"timestamp":1577512778,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDE1OlVzZXJDb250ZW50RWRpdDMzMDQ4ODMwMg=="},"target":"81f78f3cd6d3746a6f6024a14fddf43e7fd271d88550e7d560b29cde2f0ca0d5","message":"#### What is the problem?\nSuppose there two clones of a datalad superdataset in A and B, both having the subdatasets 1,2,3. If B adds a new subdataset 4, and A adds B's location as a sibling and runs `datalad update --merge --sibling B`, what happens is that A will now have subdataset 4, but cannot get the actual data. To get the data you normally run `datalad get --source B`, but this would fail because the new subdataset 4 does not have B as a remote in A. The workaround is to add manually the remote B on the newly pulled subdataset 4 inside A, then try `get` again. \n\n#### What steps will reproduce the problem?\nSorry, no code for now, above explanation should help create the situation.\n\n#### What version of DataLad are you using (run `datalad --version`)? On what operating system (consider running `datalad wtf`)?\n0.12.0rc6 from Github on Dec 2019\n\n#### Is there anything else that would be useful to know in this context?\nThough this is not a serious bug, it is confusing to the user. Moreover, it is problematic when there are hundreds of subdatasets. Running `sibling add` one by one is not reasonable, so I usually go for `sibling add --recursive` from the parent dataset. This in turn throws lots of errors for existing remotes in old subdatasets, but fixes the new ones too. The point is that the process is not smooth and shows errors. Maybe datalad can handle this situation automatically.\n\n#### Have you had any success using DataLad before? (to assess your expertise/prior luck.  We would welcome your testimonial additions to https://github.com/datalad/datalad/wiki/Testimonials as well)\nNA","files":null},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"607ac2a9afabc0e44debe80e34283e413e009770"},"timestamp":1577736514,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDU2OTc4MjQ4Mw==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/3968#issuecomment-569782483"},"message":"\u003e Sorry, no code for now, above explanation should help create the situation.\n\nI believe the minimal example below matches the situation you're describing.  I've dropped the nonessential subdatasets 1-3 and renamed the subdataset of interest, 4, to \"subds\".\n\n```sh\n#!/bin/sh\n\nset -eu\n\ncd \"$(mktemp -d --tmpdir dl-XXXXXXX)\"\n\ndatalad create A\ndatalad clone A B\ndatalad -C A siblings add --name B --url ../B\n\ndatalad -C B create -d. subds\necho foo \u003eB/subds/foo\ndatalad save -d B -r\n\ncd A\ndatalad update --merge --sibling B\ndatalad get --source B subds\n```\n\nThe final command fails with something like\n\n```\n[...]\nRemote 'B' is not available. Command failed:\nRemoteNotAvailableError: command 'annex get'\n\ninstall(ok): /tmp/dl-hKgGNo1/A/subds (dataset) [Installed subdataset in order to get /tmp/dl-hKgGNo1/A/subds]\n```\n\nNote, however, that the crucial bit is `--source B`.  That subdataset was cloned with the default remote name of \"origin\".\n\n```\nA/subds$ git remote -v                                 \norigin\t/tmp/dl-hKgGNo1/A/../B/subds (fetch)\norigin\t/tmp/dl-hKgGNo1/A/../B/subds (push)\n\nA/subds$ datalad get foo\nget(ok): foo (file) [from origin...]\n```\n\nAs far as I know, datalad doesn't provide a way to control the name of the remote that the repository was cloned from (i.e. it doesn't expose `git clone -o NAME ...`).","files":null},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"a29d5859530e551c9c63051e6b73ecbf9e195803"},"timestamp":1577743144,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDU2OTgwODUwMA==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/3968#issuecomment-569808500"},"message":"I guess the challenge for datalad is to add the remote for each newly\npulled subdataset. If a dataset is being added by a pull it is obvious that\nthe location responsible for creating the subdatasets should be a remote.\n\nOn Mon, Dec 30, 2019, 3:08 PM Kyle Meyer \u003cnotifications@github.com\u003e wrote:\n\n\u003e Sorry, no code for now, above explanation should help create the situation.\n\u003e\n\u003e I believe the minimal example below matches the situation you're\n\u003e describing. I've dropped the nonessential subdatasets 1-3 and renamed the\n\u003e subdataset of interest, 4, to \"subds\".\n\u003e\n\u003e #!/bin/sh\n\u003e set -eu\n\u003e cd \"$(mktemp -d --tmpdir dl-XXXXXXX)\"\n\u003e\n\u003e datalad create A\n\u003e datalad clone A B\n\u003e datalad -C A siblings add --name B --url ../B\n\u003e\n\u003e datalad -C B create -d. subdsecho foo \u003eB/subds/foo\n\u003e datalad save -d B -r\n\u003e cd A\n\u003e datalad update --merge --sibling B\n\u003e datalad get --source B subds\n\u003e\n\u003e The final command fails with something like\n\u003e\n\u003e [...]\n\u003e Remote 'B' is not available. Command failed:\n\u003e RemoteNotAvailableError: command 'annex get'\n\u003e\n\u003e install(ok): /tmp/dl-hKgGNo1/A/subds (dataset) [Installed subdataset in order to get /tmp/dl-hKgGNo1/A/subds]\n\u003e\n\u003e Note, however, that the crucial bit is --source B. That subdataset was\n\u003e cloned with the default remote name of \"origin\".\n\u003e\n\u003e A/subds$ git remote -v\n\u003e origin\t/tmp/dl-hKgGNo1/A/../B/subds (fetch)\n\u003e origin\t/tmp/dl-hKgGNo1/A/../B/subds (push)\n\u003e\n\u003e A/subds$ datalad get foo\n\u003e get(ok): foo (file) [from origin...]\n\u003e\n\u003e As far as I know, datalad doesn't provide a way to control the name of the\n\u003e remote that the repository was cloned from (i.e. it doesn't expose git\n\u003e clone -o NAME ...).\n\u003e\n\u003e —\n\u003e You are receiving this because you authored the thread.\n\u003e Reply to this email directly, view it on GitHub\n\u003e \u003chttps://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/3968?email_source=notifications\u0026email_token=ACFJU7LIS5HJT77NGEDBSVDQ3JIMHA5CNFSM4KAHSQLKYY3PNVWWK3TUL52HS4DFVREXG43VMVBW63LNMVXHJKTDN5WW2ZLOORPWSZGOEH3DBUY#issuecomment-569782483\u003e,\n\u003e or unsubscribe\n\u003e \u003chttps://github.com/notifications/unsubscribe-auth/ACFJU7IF7TAVZOCC3NSJM4TQ3JIMHANCNFSM4KAHSQLA\u003e\n\u003e .\n\u003e","files":null},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"a29d5859530e551c9c63051e6b73ecbf9e195803"},"timestamp":1577743241,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDU2OTgwODg5OA==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/3968#issuecomment-569808898"},"message":"BTW, has the master for 0.12.0 switched from \"install\" to \"clone\"?\n\nOn Mon, Dec 30, 2019, 4:58 PM Dorian Pustina \u003calbnet@gmail.com\u003e wrote:\n\n\u003e I guess the challenge for datalad is to add the remote for each newly\n\u003e pulled subdataset. If a dataset is being added by a pull it is obvious that\n\u003e the location responsible for creating the subdatasets should be a remote.\n\u003e\n\u003e On Mon, Dec 30, 2019, 3:08 PM Kyle Meyer \u003cnotifications@github.com\u003e wrote:\n\u003e\n\u003e\u003e Sorry, no code for now, above explanation should help create the\n\u003e\u003e situation.\n\u003e\u003e\n\u003e\u003e I believe the minimal example below matches the situation you're\n\u003e\u003e describing. I've dropped the nonessential subdatasets 1-3 and renamed the\n\u003e\u003e subdataset of interest, 4, to \"subds\".\n\u003e\u003e\n\u003e\u003e #!/bin/sh\n\u003e\u003e set -eu\n\u003e\u003e cd \"$(mktemp -d --tmpdir dl-XXXXXXX)\"\n\u003e\u003e\n\u003e\u003e datalad create A\n\u003e\u003e datalad clone A B\n\u003e\u003e datalad -C A siblings add --name B --url ../B\n\u003e\u003e\n\u003e\u003e datalad -C B create -d. subdsecho foo \u003eB/subds/foo\n\u003e\u003e datalad save -d B -r\n\u003e\u003e cd A\n\u003e\u003e datalad update --merge --sibling B\n\u003e\u003e datalad get --source B subds\n\u003e\u003e\n\u003e\u003e The final command fails with something like\n\u003e\u003e\n\u003e\u003e [...]\n\u003e\u003e Remote 'B' is not available. Command failed:\n\u003e\u003e RemoteNotAvailableError: command 'annex get'\n\u003e\u003e\n\u003e\u003e install(ok): /tmp/dl-hKgGNo1/A/subds (dataset) [Installed subdataset in order to get /tmp/dl-hKgGNo1/A/subds]\n\u003e\u003e\n\u003e\u003e Note, however, that the crucial bit is --source B. That subdataset was\n\u003e\u003e cloned with the default remote name of \"origin\".\n\u003e\u003e\n\u003e\u003e A/subds$ git remote -v\n\u003e\u003e origin\t/tmp/dl-hKgGNo1/A/../B/subds (fetch)\n\u003e\u003e origin\t/tmp/dl-hKgGNo1/A/../B/subds (push)\n\u003e\u003e\n\u003e\u003e A/subds$ datalad get foo\n\u003e\u003e get(ok): foo (file) [from origin...]\n\u003e\u003e\n\u003e\u003e As far as I know, datalad doesn't provide a way to control the name of\n\u003e\u003e the remote that the repository was cloned from (i.e. it doesn't expose git\n\u003e\u003e clone -o NAME ...).\n\u003e\u003e\n\u003e\u003e —\n\u003e\u003e You are receiving this because you authored the thread.\n\u003e\u003e Reply to this email directly, view it on GitHub\n\u003e\u003e \u003chttps://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/3968?email_source=notifications\u0026email_token=ACFJU7LIS5HJT77NGEDBSVDQ3JIMHA5CNFSM4KAHSQLKYY3PNVWWK3TUL52HS4DFVREXG43VMVBW63LNMVXHJKTDN5WW2ZLOORPWSZGOEH3DBUY#issuecomment-569782483\u003e,\n\u003e\u003e or unsubscribe\n\u003e\u003e \u003chttps://github.com/notifications/unsubscribe-auth/ACFJU7IF7TAVZOCC3NSJM4TQ3JIMHANCNFSM4KAHSQLA\u003e\n\u003e\u003e .\n\u003e\u003e\n\u003e","files":null},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"bfb0c87a087f143d5d4fb3c3b303347e581f98d0"},"timestamp":1577745299,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDU2OTgxNjA5Mg==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/3968#issuecomment-569816092"},"message":"Not really. Just in some use cases/ handbook it is used instead as the least common denominator for needed functionality","files":null},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"607ac2a9afabc0e44debe80e34283e413e009770"},"timestamp":1577747264,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDU2OTgyMjUyNw==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/3968#issuecomment-569822527"},"message":"\u003e I guess the challenge for datalad is to add the remote for each newly pulled subdataset.\n\nThe url for the clone is being added as a remote.  It's just that it's named \"origin\".\n\n\u003e If a dataset is being added by a pull it is obvious that the location responsible for creating the subdatasets should be a remote.\n\nHmm, I think you're imagining a tighter link than there actually is between the sibling used in the `update` call and the clone url used when you call `get` on a subdataset.  Commits that register a new submodule may come from any remote that the user happens to fetch from, but that doesn't mean that the subdataset has a url that is based on the superdataset's remote.  `get` assembles a list of clone url candidates based on things like the configured remote for that branch, which remotes have the HEAD commit, and `get.subdataset-source-candidate-*` configuration values (see [get.py:_get_flexible_source_candidates_for_submodule][0] for more details).\n\nWe _could_ update `clone` to accept a name other than \"origin\" (i.e expose `git clone -o NAME ...`) and teach `get` to feed in the remote name to its clone call.  But I don't understand what the benefit of that would be, and a downside is that it would make the name of the added remote less predictable (it'd depend on the order in which remotes are tried, among other things).\n\n[0]: https://github.com/datalad/datalad/blob/ea4a9f8aff38f4441c8c2c5fa95a51dbc256720d/datalad/distribution/get.py#L98-L136","files":null},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"a29d5859530e551c9c63051e6b73ecbf9e195803"},"timestamp":1577751961,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDU2OTgzNTcxNw==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/3968#issuecomment-569835717"},"message":"Sorry but I don't know what update uses in the background. What I meant is\nthat the newly pulled dataset should not have origin as remote but the\nremote it is coming from. My master dataset, for example, does not have a\nremote called origin, just ad hoc remotes/siblings that are added and\nremoved as needed.\n\nIf an update from custom remote creates new siblings with a remote origin,\nit might be quite complicated to keep track of remotes. Particularly if\nexisting datasets already have an origin which points elsewhere, so origin\nwill be heterogeneous after the merge.\n\nOn Mon, Dec 30, 2019, 6:07 PM Kyle Meyer \u003cnotifications@github.com\u003e wrote:\n\n\u003e I guess the challenge for datalad is to add the remote for each newly\n\u003e pulled subdataset.\n\u003e\n\u003e The url for the clone is being added as a remote. It's just that it's\n\u003e named \"origin\".\n\u003e\n\u003e If a dataset is being added by a pull it is obvious that the location\n\u003e responsible for creating the subdatasets should be a remote.\n\u003e\n\u003e Hmm, I think you're imagining a tighter link than there actually is\n\u003e between the sibling used in the update call and the clone url used when\n\u003e you call get on a subdataset. Commits that register a new submodule may\n\u003e come from any remote that the user happens to fetch from, but that doesn't\n\u003e mean that the subdataset has a url that is based on the superdataset's\n\u003e remote. get assembles a list of clone url candidates based on things like\n\u003e the configured remote for that branch, which remotes have the HEAD commit,\n\u003e and get.subdataset-source-candidate-* configuration values (see\n\u003e get.py:_get_flexible_source_candidates_for_submodule\n\u003e \u003chttps://github.com/datalad/datalad/blob/ea4a9f8aff38f4441c8c2c5fa95a51dbc256720d/datalad/distribution/get.py#L98-L136\u003e\n\u003e for more details).\n\u003e\n\u003e We *could* update clone to accept a name other than \"origin\" (i.e expose git\n\u003e clone -o NAME ...) and teach get to feed in the remote name to its clone\n\u003e call. But I don't understand what the benefit of that would be, and a\n\u003e downside is that it would make the name of the added remote less\n\u003e predictable (it'd depend on the order in which remotes are tried, among\n\u003e other things).\n\u003e\n\u003e —\n\u003e You are receiving this because you authored the thread.\n\u003e Reply to this email directly, view it on GitHub\n\u003e \u003chttps://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/3968?email_source=notifications\u0026email_token=ACFJU7KZRYDMZG2Y4KWGT5TQ3J5MBA5CNFSM4KAHSQLKYY3PNVWWK3TUL52HS4DFVREXG43VMVBW63LNMVXHJKTDN5WW2ZLOORPWSZGOEH3M2PY#issuecomment-569822527\u003e,\n\u003e or unsubscribe\n\u003e \u003chttps://github.com/notifications/unsubscribe-auth/ACFJU7LQQ7MT5SXLW54PRALQ3J5MBANCNFSM4KAHSQLA\u003e\n\u003e .\n\u003e","files":null},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"607ac2a9afabc0e44debe80e34283e413e009770"},"timestamp":1578668046,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDU3MzA2NjU5OQ==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/3968#issuecomment-573066599"},"message":"As I mentioned at gh-4005, I think this issue is the same root issue as that one, so I'll close this (though please reopen if I'm mistaken).","files":null},{"type":4,"author":{"id":"607ac2a9afabc0e44debe80e34283e413e009770"},"timestamp":1578668047,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDExOkNsb3NlZEV2ZW50MjkzODc3ODA1NA=="},"status":2}]}